WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.480
In the early 1980s, neonatal intensive
care units across the United States

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.600
were fighting a constant battle to keep
low birth weight premature infants

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:14.480
alive. Part of the standard protocol
involved delivering essential fluids and

4
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.920
medications through intravenous lines.

5
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.380
To keep those IV solutions sterile,
doctors relied on a common,

6
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.860
everyday preservative called benzyl
alcohol.

7
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.340
The medical community knew the preservative
was safe, but that confidence was

8
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.540
built on a massive, unchecked assumption
that you could take physiological

9
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.560
safety data collected from healthy adults
and apply it directly to newborns.

10
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.760
Biologically, that equation fails.

11
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.220
While adult bodies break down benzyl
alcohol through mature metabolic

12
00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.360
pathways, premature infants haven't
developed the liver function required to

13
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.980
process it. Because the pathway is
blocked, the chemical builds up rapidly in

14
00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.100
the infant's bloodstream, turning a
routine safety measure into a concentrated

15
00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:01.860
toxin. This physiological failure led to
severe respiratory distress,

16
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.100
organ failure, and a wave of fatalities
that came to be known as the gasping

17
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.000
syndrome. The doctors prescribing those
fluids were trying to save lives,

18
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:17.000
but good intentions cannot replace
population-specific safeguards.

19
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.560
Assuming a vulnerable patient is just a
smaller version of a healthy adult is

20
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.780
a devastating ethical failure.
